.TH TTSNOOP 1 "2026-11-22" "Amiga UNIX add-on" "CDE"
.SH NAME
ttsnoop \- watch and send ToolTalk messages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ttsnoop
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR scopefile ]
.RB [ \-\e<
.IR procid ]
.RB [ \-v
.IR media ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR op ]
.br
.B ttsnoop
.RI [ options ]
.BR \-n | \-N
.br
.B ttsnoop
.RI [ options ]
.RB [ \-e
.IR script ]
.I command
.RI [ argument " ...]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ttsnoop
is a window onto a ToolTalk session.
It joins the session, registers a pattern that matches every message
(narrowed by the options below), and shows each message it sees in a
terminal pane, with its operation, class, scope, state, sender and
arguments.
Its menus build and send messages and patterns, show the session's
ptypes and otypes, and turn on
.BR tttrace (1)
tracing; from a message shown it can make the
.I TT_MSG
action that would send it.
.PP
Given a
.IR command ,
it runs it under
.BR tttrace (1)
and shows the ToolTalk calls the command makes.
.SS The initial pattern
.TP 16
.BI \-F " scopefile"
also match messages scoped to
.I scopefile
.TP
.BI \-\e< " procid"
only messages from
.I procid
.TP
.BI \-v " media"
only messages for media type
.I media
.TP
.BI \-m " op"
only messages with operation
.I op
.TP
.B \-n
register no initial pattern
.TP
.B \-N
register none and do not join the session at start-up either
.SS Options
.TP 16
.B \-T
trace ttsnoop's own ToolTalk calls, from the start
.TP
.B \-u
de-iconify the window when a message arrives
.TP
.BI \-S " session"
the session to join (default: the one in
.BR TT_SESSION ,
or the display's)
.TP
.B \-X
join the X display's session
.TP
.BI \-w " n"
time out calls after
.I n
seconds (default 20)
.TP
.BI \-l " n"
keep
.I n
lines of output in the pane (default 5000)
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
also write the messages seen to
.I file
.TP
.BI \-O " file"
also write the call trace to
.I file
.TP
.BI \-e " script"
the
.BR tttrace (1)
setting for
.IR command ,
as
.BR tttracefile (4)
describes
.SH EXIT STATUS
2 for a usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ttsession (1),
.BR tttrace (1)
